    "['set protocols isis reference-bandwidth [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router isis', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'router isis', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco command for the Juniper `set protocols isis reference-bandwidth 1000000` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter isis\\n metric-style wide\\n is-type level-1\\n reference-bandwidth 1000\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Cisco, the reference-bandwidth is configured in Mbps (1000 Mbps = 1 Gbps), while Juniper uses bps (1000000 bps = 1 Mbps)\\n2. I've included the `metric-style wide` and `is-type level-1` commands to match the Juniper configuration's `wide-metrics-only` and level-1 settings\\n3. The actual equivalent bandwidth value would be 1000 in Cisco to match Juniper's 1000000 (1Gbps)\", 'trans_template': 'router isis'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'metric-style wide', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'router isis [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco command for the Juniper `set protocols isis reference-bandwidth 1000000` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter isis\\n metric-style wide\\n is-type level-1\\n reference-bandwidth 1000\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Cisco, the reference-bandwidth is configured in Mbps (1000 Mbps = 1 Gbps), while Juniper uses bps (1000000 bps = 1 Mbps)\\n2. I've included the `metric-style wide` and `is-type level-1` commands to match the Juniper configuration's `wide-metrics-only` and level-1 settings\\n3. The actual equivalent bandwidth value would be 1000 in Cisco to match Juniper's 1000000 (1Gbps)\", 'trans_template': 'metric-style wide'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'is-type level-1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'router isis [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco command for the Juniper `set protocols isis reference-bandwidth 1000000` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter isis\\n metric-style wide\\n is-type level-1\\n reference-bandwidth 1000\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Cisco, the reference-bandwidth is configured in Mbps (1000 Mbps = 1 Gbps), while Juniper uses bps (1000000 bps = 1 Mbps)\\n2. I've included the `metric-style wide` and `is-type level-1` commands to match the Juniper configuration's `wide-metrics-only` and level-1 settings\\n3. The actual equivalent bandwidth value would be 1000 in Cisco to match Juniper's 1000000 (1Gbps)\", 'trans_template': 'is-type level-1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'reference-bandwidth 1000', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'reference-bandwidth 1000', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco command for the Juniper `set protocols isis reference-bandwidth 1000000` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter isis\\n metric-style wide\\n is-type level-1\\n reference-bandwidth 1000\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Cisco, the reference-bandwidth is configured in Mbps (1000 Mbps = 1 Gbps), while Juniper uses bps (1000000 bps = 1 Mbps)\\n2. I've included the `metric-style wide` and `is-type level-1` commands to match the Juniper configuration's `wide-metrics-only` and level-1 settings\\n3. The actual equivalent bandwidth value would be 1000 in Cisco to match Juniper's 1000000 (1Gbps)\", 'trans_template': 'reference-bandwidth 1000'}]]": 1,
    "['set policy-options policy-statement [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'interface Loopback0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface Loopback0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translation of the Juniper command `set protocols isis interface lo0.0 passive` to Cisco IOS:\\n\\n##\\ninterface Loopback0\\n ip router isis\\n isis passive\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, `lo0.0 passive` makes the loopback interface passive in IS-IS (it's advertised but doesn't form adjacencies)\\n- In Cisco, we first enable IS-IS on the interface with `ip router isis` (assuming IS-IS is already configured globally)\\n- Then we set it as passive with `isis passive` command\\n- The Loopback0 interface is the Cisco equivalent of Juniper's lo0.0\", 'trans_template': 'interface Loopback0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ip router isis', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translation of the Juniper command `set protocols isis interface lo0.0 passive` to Cisco IOS:\\n\\n##\\ninterface Loopback0\\n ip router isis\\n isis passive\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, `lo0.0 passive` makes the loopback interface passive in IS-IS (it's advertised but doesn't form adjacencies)\\n- In Cisco, we first enable IS-IS on the interface with `ip router isis` (assuming IS-IS is already configured globally)\\n- Then we set it as passive with `isis passive` command\\n- The Loopback0 interface is the Cisco equivalent of Juniper's lo0.0\", 'trans_template': 'ip router isis'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'isis passive', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translation of the Juniper command `set protocols isis interface lo0.0 passive` to Cisco IOS:\\n\\n##\\ninterface Loopback0\\n ip router isis\\n isis passive\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, `lo0.0 passive` makes the loopback interface passive in IS-IS (it's advertised but doesn't form adjacencies)\\n- In Cisco, we first enable IS-IS on the interface with `ip router isis` (assuming IS-IS is already configured globally)\\n- Then we set it as passive with `isis passive` command\\n- The Loopback0 interface is the Cisco equivalent of Juniper's lo0.0\", 'trans_template': 'isis passive'}]]": 1,
    "['term [parameter2] from protocol [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'route-map REDIST-CONNECTED permit 10', 'parent_command': ['interface Loopback0'], 'root': 'interface Loopback0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map REDIST-CONNECTED permit 10\\n match interface *\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, `policy-statement REDIST-CONNECTED term 1 from protocol direct` matches directly connected routes\\n- The Cisco equivalent uses a route-map with `match interface *` to accomplish the same thing\\n- The `permit 10` corresponds to `term 1` in the Juniper configuration\\n- Both achieve the same goal of matching directly connected routes for redistribution\", 'trans_template': 'route-map REDIST-CONNECTED permit 10'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'match interface *', 'parent_command': ['interface Loopback0'], 'root': 'interface Loopback0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map REDIST-CONNECTED permit 10\\n match interface *\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, `policy-statement REDIST-CONNECTED term 1 from protocol direct` matches directly connected routes\\n- The Cisco equivalent uses a route-map with `match interface *` to accomplish the same thing\\n- The `permit 10` corresponds to `term 1` in the Juniper configuration\\n- Both achieve the same goal of matching directly connected routes for redistribution\", 'trans_template': 'match interface *'}]]": 1,
